<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- WinExe (not Exe) so no console window flashes on screen at logon. -->
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>

    <!--
      The SDK-versioned TFM is what supplies the WinRT projections for
      Windows.Devices.Bluetooth, .Enumeration and .Radios, which this is built on.
      It adds no package reference.

      **net9.0, alone among these utilities.**

      Every WinRT call here that takes a *collection* of property-key strings
      failed at run time under AOT:

        Failed to create a CCW for object of type 'System.String[]'
        for interface with IID 'E2FCC7C1-3BFC-5A0B-B2B0-72E769D1CB7E'

      That IID is IIterable<String>. Handing a managed array to WinRT needs a
      COM-callable wrapper, and building one at run time needs code generation -
      the one thing ahead-of-time compilation removes. Both device watchers,
      DeviceInformation.FindAllAsync and PnpObject.FindAllAsync all go through
      such a call, so the utility saw the radio and could never see a device.
      Radio.GetRadiosAsync() kept working because it is the only WinRT call in
      the codebase that takes no arguments.

      net9.0's projections carry CsWinRT's AOT support, which emits these vtables
      at compile time. The cost is real but bounded: the ILCompiler package
      follows the target framework rather than the installed SDK, so this pulls a
      net9.0 ILC alongside the net8.0 one the rest of this suite uses. The
      binary is framework-independent either way.
    -->
    <TargetFramework>net9.0-windows10.0.19041.0</TargetFramework>
    <SupportedOSPlatformVersion>10.0.19041.0</SupportedOSPlatformVersion>
    <RuntimeIdentifier>win-x64</RuntimeIdentifier>

    <!--
      The OS-versioned TFM above makes the SDK refuse to resolve the Windows
      reference assemblies on a non-Windows machine unless this is set
      explicitly (NETSDK1100). It changes nothing on a real Windows machine,
      where the OS actually matches; it is Microsoft's documented way to let a
      Windows-targeted TFM restore and compile (metadata-only, never link or
      run) from a non-Windows SDK, for type-checking a build elsewhere.
    -->
    <EnableWindowsTargeting>true</EnableWindowsTargeting>
    <AssemblyName>BluetoothBattery</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>BluetoothBattery</RootNamespace>

    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>

    <!--
      What Windows shows a person, as opposed to a filename. AssemblyTitle becomes
      the Win32 FileDescription, which is what Task Manager reads for its Name
      column and Explorer shows in Properties -> Details.
    -->
    <AssemblyTitle>Bluetooth Battery</AssemblyTitle>
    <Product>Bluetooth Battery</Product>
    <Version>1.0.0</Version>
    <FileVersion>1.0.0.0</FileVersion>

    <!--
      The Win32 version resource is authored in version.rc rather than generated
      from the properties above. The generated one is always language-neutral -
      Properties > Details reads "Language Independent" - and no MSBuild property
      changes that. The strings above still feed the *managed* assembly
      attributes, so keep the two in step.
    -->
    <Win32Resource>version.res</Win32Resource>

    <!--
      Ahead-of-time compiled to a native binary. Avoiding UseWindowsForms keeps
      this small - a WinForms build drags in the whole desktop runtime and
      blocks trimming outright, where this configuration measures at 4.2 MB
      with no loose DLLs beside it.

      SelfContained and single-file are implied by AOT and must not be set
      alongside it.
    -->
    <PublishAot>true</PublishAot>
    <IsAotCompatible>true</IsAotCompatible>

    <!--
      The generator that fixes the CCW failure above. It scans for managed types
      handed to WinRT and emits their vtables into the binary, so nothing has to
      be built at run time.

      Set explicitly rather than left to its default, because the whole port
      depends on it and a silent default flip would be invisible until the device
      list came back empty again.
    -->
    <CsWinRTAotOptimizerEnabled>true</CsWinRTAotOptimizerEnabled>

    <!--
      **The analyser that would have caught the CCW failure above before it
      shipped.**

      Level 1 is the default when PublishAot is set, and level 1 says nothing
      about any of it. Level 2 reports every type that implements a WinRT
      interface without being partial - the same failure in a different place:
      a vtable the runtime would have to build for itself, at a point where it
      cannot. Worth keeping at 2, since that failure is otherwise silent at
      build time and silent at publish time, only visible as an empty device
      list at run time.
    -->
    <CsWinRTAotWarningLevel>2</CsWinRTAotWarningLevel>

    <!--
      InvariantTimezone is deliberately NOT set, though AOT guides suggest it as a
      size saving. It strips the timezone database and DateTime.Now then falls back
      to UTC - every log line would silently shift by the local offset and stop
      lining up with the other utilities' logs.
    -->
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      **The one package reference in this project, and the only one in the suite.**

      This project's goal is no third-party dependencies, and this does not
      breach it in any sense with consequences at run time:

        - It is first-party Microsoft, not third-party.
        - Under AOT it is a **build-time input**. The compiler links what is used
          into the executable; the publish directory contains the exe and
          nothing else. Nothing ships beside the binary and nothing is loaded at
          run time.

      It exists because DevicePopup draws text - the one thing that needed
      proving before this port was attempted, since nothing else here draws a
      single character.

      Pinned rather than floating. Tracks the target framework (9.0.x rather
      than 8.0.x) - mixing them pulls the 8.0 assembly in through a
      compatibility path for no benefit.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="System.Drawing.Common" Version="9.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
